{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01WLEkbiJDKwn699d3CFHSp7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so I can use `lemma_map_size` to relate the size of `{k: NodeId | k < n}` to `{k: int | 0 <= k < n}` via the map `f = |k: NodeId| k as int`. Combined with `lemma_int_range(0, n as int)`, I can get the size of the NodeId range set.\n\nBut I also need to show that `f` is injective on the NodeId set. For u32, the cast to int is injective. And I need to show that `{k: NodeId | k < n}.map(|k: NodeId| k as int)` equals `{k: int | 0 <= k < n}`, which is `set_int_range(0, n as int)`.\n \nActually, I think the inductive approach is cleaner here. I'll follow the same pattern as `lemma_int_range`, using the observation that I can build up the range set by inserting elements one at a time. The key is handling the induction on u32 values, which requires careful reasoning about the bounds.\n\nWriting the proof function... I'm verifying the set equality by checking that inserting the new element into the previous set produces the same result as the original set, since both contain exactly the elements where the index is less than n. proof body `{assert(s_prev.insert(m) =~= s);}` might not work by itself. I need to add the forall assertion:\n\nI'm writing a proof that unfolds the set operations using their axioms. The SMT solver should handle the equivalence between `s_prev.insert(m).contains(k)` and `s.contains(k)` by recognizing that inserting element `(n-1)` into a set of elements less than `(n-1)` is equivalent to a set of elements less than `n` — this reduces to pure arithmetic reasoning.\n\nFor the assertion that `m` isn't already in `s_prev`, the solver should automatically derive that `(n-1) < (n-1)` is false. Then I can use the set insertion length axiom to establish that inserting `m` increases the length by exactly one.\n\nSince `s` is equivalent to `s_prev.insert(m)`, the length of `s` becomes `s_prev.len() + 1`, which by the inductive hypothesis equals `n`. For finiteness, I need to verify that the insert operation preserves the finite property — there should be an axiom in vstd that handles this, or it follows transitively from the length axiom.\n\nLooking at how `lemma_int_range` handles the same pattern, it relies on Verus to automatically discharge the finiteness goal after applying the insert operation, so I'll follow that approach even though I'm working with `NodeId` sets instead of `int` sets. there's an axiom `axiom_set_insert_finite` in vstd, or it's implied by the broadcast lemma `group_set_properties`. The proof of `lemma_int_range` works, so I'll trust that the same approach works for u32.\n\nLooking at `lemma_int_range` more carefully, it doesn't actually use `broadcast use` — just the recursive call and set equality assertion is enough for Verus to verify. That's the same pattern I need.\n\nSo here's my plan: add a helper `lemma_range_nodeid_len(n: u32)`, then before the while loop assert that the empty subrange equals an empty set. In the proof for Case A, I'll construct a universe set and use the helper lemma to establish the cardinality bound.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the assertion `(k as int) < n as int` will follow automatically from the existing constraints... Still writing proof logic... iated at index `j`. Since `0 <= j < pref_new.len() = i as int <= voters@.len()`, this gives `(voters@[j] as int) < n as int`. The forall in `all_in_range` should be instantiated by Z3 automatically given `j` is in range.\n\nLet me check the argument order for `lemma_len_subset`. It takes two sets and requires the first to be a subset of the second, giving us that their lengths satisfy the subset relationship. So calling it with `pref_new.to_set()` and `universe` should work—I need `universe` to be finite (which I have from `lemma_range_nodeid_len`) and `pref_new.to_set()` to be a subset of `universe` (which I've asserted). This gives me `pref_new.to_set().len() <= universe.len()`, and combined with `universe.len() == n as nat`, I can conclude that `count <= n as nat`.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the equality `count == pref_new.to_set().len()` that I asserted earlier at line 239 is still available when I reach line 254—both assertions are in the same scope, so the fact should carry through.\n\nTo be explicit, I'm adding that equality directly into the proof block for the count bound, then establishing that the preference set is a subset of the universe by showing each element in the set satisfies the range constraint.\n\nStill writing proof logic... I'm working through the assertion structure and realizing that after establishing `count == pref_new.to_set().len()`, I need to prove `count <= n`, but the solver doesn't automatically know that the set length is bounded by `n` without the range set argument. Let me figure out what lemmas I actually need to invoke here. I need to verify whether that axiom actually exists in the vstd library.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2002,"cache_read_input_tokens":59573,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2002},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"344ae076-d406-465e-a149-63b85155dae0","uuid":"fbfabca7-5e91-4263-a7ad-5a38ade9c439"}
